    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Ocean Colour Scene’s self-titled 1992 debut captures the Birmingham band in its pre-Britpop, post-Madchester phase, blending rock with R&B, shoegaze, and soul influences under producer Brendan Lynch. Released on Fontana, it showed a more textured and exploratory sound than the group’s later, more anthemic records, with songs that lean on groove, atmosphere, and melodic guitar interplay rather than the widescreen swagger that would define their mid-1990s breakthrough.\n\nAlthough it did not become a major hit at the time, the album established the band’s core strengths: tight musicianship, a classicist sense of songcraft, and a taste for warm, retro-leaning British rock. It was later overshadowed by the success of Moseley Shoals, but the debut has remained significant as the first statement from a band that would soon become one of the defining guitar acts of the Britpop era.",
